# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Rousing the Officers — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Chapter Six — Rousing the Officers'
HEAD = '''Five passages, the shortest chapter in the book and the only one that ends in a real battle. Marquis Wu opens by asking whether severe punishments and clear rewards are enough to win with. Wu Qi’s answer turns on one sentence: <b>“Of severity and clarity your servant cannot speak fully; but they are not the thing to rely on.”</b> What can be relied on is three things: that when the orders go out men are glad to hear them, that when the army is raised men are glad to fight, and that when the blades cross men are glad to die. Three gladnesses.

How is that brought about? Not by raising the reward. By <b>giving a dinner in the courtyard of the ancestral temple</b>. Three rows of mats: those of the first rank of service in the front row, with precious vessels and the best meat besides their food; the second rank in the middle row, their vessels less; those with no service at all in the back row, food and no precious vessels. When the feast breaks up, presents are given outside the temple gate to the parents, wives and children of those who had served, again by rank. And to the households of men killed in service, an envoy is sent every year to comfort and reward the parents — <b>to make it plain that they have not been forgotten</b>.

After three years of this the men of Qin raised an army and came up to the West River, and the men of Wei, without waiting for an order from any official, put on armour and went at them, tens of thousands of them. Marquis Wu sent for Wu Qi to say that his method had worked, and Wu Qi’s reply is the edge of the chapter: give me fifty thousand men who have no record of service and let me take the Qin army with them. <b>“Set one desperate bandit in open country and a thousand men will go after him, and every one of them will look about him like an owl and turn his head like a wolf”</b> — afraid the man may spring up and do him some harm. And so one man who has thrown his life away is enough to frighten a thousand. The marquis agreed, added five hundred chariots and three thousand horse, and broke a Qin host of five hundred thousand.

The last passage is the order issued the day before the battle, and it is three sentences long: every officer and man is to take on the enemy of his own kind — chariot against chariot, horse against horse, foot against foot — and “if the chariots do not get chariots, the horse do not get horse and the foot do not get foot, then even if we break their army, none of you shall have any credit.” And so on the day, “his orders were not many, and his authority shook the world.”'''

PARA = {
 'p6-1': 'Marquis Wu asked: “Are severe punishments and clear rewards enough to bring victory?” Wu Qi replied: “Of severity and clarity your servant cannot speak fully. Even so, they are not the thing to rely on. What can be relied on is this: that when the word goes out and the order is given, men are glad to hear it; that when the army is raised and the multitude is moved, men are glad to fight; that when blade meets blade, men are glad to die. These three are what a ruler may rely on.” (“Cannot speak fully” is a courteous way of putting the question aside: Wu Qi does not say that severe punishment and clear reward are wrong, only that they are not the root of the thing. The four words “they are not the thing to rely on” are where the chapter starts. The three gladnesses deepen by a step each time — glad to hear is obedience, glad to fight is taking the field, glad to die is not turning back. The Siku text reads “when the word goes out and the order is <i>issued</i>.”)',
 'p6-2': 'Marquis Wu said: “How is this brought about?” The reply: “Let my lord single out those who have done service and feast them; and those who have done none, let him urge them on.” (To “feast” is to entertain with food and drink as a mark of honour. To “urge on” is to encourage, not to punish — and that is where the chapter takes its title, Rousing the Officers. It is the shortest passage in the book, and it states the whole method in outline: a dinner for those who have served and, for those who have not, no reproach but a spur. The passage that follows gives the dinner in detail.)',
 'p6-3': 'Thereupon Marquis Wu had mats set out in the courtyard of the ancestral temple, in three rows, and feasted his officers. Those of the first rank of service sat in the front row, and beside their food they had precious vessels and the best meat; those of the second rank sat in the middle row, their food and their vessels less; those with no service sat in the back row, with food but no precious vessels. When the feast broke up and they came out, presents were made outside the temple gate to the parents, wives and children of those who had done service, again graded by rank. And to the households of those who had died in service an envoy was sent every year to comfort and reward their parents, making it plain that they were not forgotten. This had been done for three years when the men of Qin raised an army and came up to the West River. The men of Wei heard of it, and without waiting for any official’s order put on helmet and armour and went at them; there were tens of thousands of them. (The courtyard is that of the ancestral temple, so that where a man sat was settled in front of the ancestors. “The best meat” is the highest grade of sacrificial animal; the “precious vessels” are ritual bronzes. “Making it plain that they were not forgotten” is to give the remembering a visible form. What matters here is not the size of the reward but that the whole of it is <i>public</i>: the seating is public, the presents to parents and wives at the temple gate are public, the yearly envoy is public. And what is really being shown to the living is the phrase “households of those who had died in service” — men will spend their lives when they can see that the families of the dead are looked after. For “helmet” the base text has the graph meaning a helmet and both witnesses the graph meaning a descendant; the two are easily confused and the base text is right. The Sibu congkan text reads “every year <i>an envoy</i> was sent” with a different construction and the Siku text “every year <i>despatched</i> an envoy”; the base text’s doubled graph, “sent a messenger,” is kept.)',
 'p6-4': 'Marquis Wu summoned Wu Qi and said to him: “Your teaching of the other day has taken effect.” Wu Qi replied: “Your servant has heard that men have their strong points and their weak, and that spirit has its times of vigour and of decay. Let my lord try issuing me fifty thousand men who have no record of service, and your servant asks leave to lead them against the army of Qin. If we should fail to win, we shall be laughed at by the feudal lords and lose our authority in the world. Now set one desperate bandit in open country, and let a thousand men go after him: there will not be one of them who does not look about him like an owl and turn his head like a wolf. Why? Because each is afraid that the man may spring up and do him harm. So one man who has thrown his life away is enough to frighten a thousand. Now if your servant takes these fifty thousand and makes of them that one desperate bandit, and leads them to punish Qin, they will indeed be hard to face.” Thereupon Marquis Wu did as he asked, adding five hundred chariots and three thousand horse, and broke a host of five hundred thousand men of Qin. Such was the effect of rousing the officers. (“If we should fail” renders a word meaning “supposing, by any chance.” “Look about him like an owl and turn his head like a wolf” describes the pursuers, not the pursued: it is the thousand who are frightened. “Thrown his life away” is to have staked it and let it go. The figure of five hundred thousand is this book’s; the <i>Records of the Historian</i> does not give it. Note that what Wu Qi asks for is the fifty thousand <i>without</i> service — the men who sat in the back row at the dinner. “One man who has thrown his life away is enough to frighten a thousand” is where the three gladnesses of the first passage are cashed in. The Sibu congkan text reads “because each <i>begrudges</i> that he may spring up.”)',
 'p6-5': 'On the day before the battle Wu Qi gave orders to the army, saying: “All officers and men are to take on the enemy of their own sort — chariots, horse and foot. If the chariots do not get the enemy’s chariots, the horse do not get his horse and the foot do not get his foot, then though we break their army, none shall have any credit.” And so on the day of battle his orders were few and his authority shook the world. (“Take on the enemy of their own sort” means that each arm is to answer for the corresponding arm opposite. “The chariots do not get the enemy’s chariots” means the charioteer has not taken a chariot of theirs. The book ends on this single order, and the order redefines merit: not whether the army won, but whether you closed with your own opposite number. “His orders were few” answers the fifth of the commander’s five cares in chapter four, “restraint is to keep the regulations few and unfussy.” After forty-two passages of argument, what the book leaves the reader with is one sentence that anybody can understand and nobody can slip out of. The Sibu congkan text miswrites both “take on the enemy” and “have any credit.”)',
}
